      "id": 1,
      "text": "Marysville was founded in 1855 by Francis J. and became the county seat of Marshall County the same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Marshall’s wife, a Border Ruffian, and the city was once known for its whiskey and shooting affrays.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Marysville sits on the historic Oregon Trail and was a stop for the Pony Express, whose original station still stands downtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marysville%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Koester House Museum and Gardens showcase a living legacy of local architecture and storybook gardens, drawing visitors into Marysville’s past.",
